---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import BodyFatCalculator from '@components/islands/BodyFatCalculator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';

const calc = getCalculator('health', 'body-fat-calculator')!;

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'How does the U.S. Navy body fat method work?',
    answer:
      'It estimates body fat from body-circumference measurements — neck and waist for men, plus hips for women — together with your height. It is quick, needs only a tape measure, and correlates reasonably well with more expensive methods.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Where exactly do I measure?',
    answer:
      'Measure your neck just below the larynx, your waist at the navel, and (for women) your hips at the widest point. Keep the tape snug but not compressing the skin, and measure without holding your breath.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is a healthy body fat percentage?',
    answer:
      'General ranges differ by sex. For men, roughly 6–24% spans athletic to average; for women, about 14–31%. Essential fat is around 2–5% for men and 10–13% for women. Above these ranges is generally classed as obese.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How accurate is it?',
    answer:
      'The Navy method is an estimate, not a clinical measurement. It can be off by several points for individuals. For precise figures, methods like DEXA scanning are more reliable.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What is the difference between body fat percentage and BMI?',
    answer:
      'Body fat percentage measures how much of your weight is fat, directly. BMI is only a ratio of weight to height and cannot tell fat from muscle, so a muscular person can have a high BMI but low body fat. Body fat percentage is the more informative of the two.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How can I reduce my body fat percentage?',
    answer:
      'A sustained, moderate calorie deficit combined with resistance training and adequate protein is the established approach — the training and protein help preserve muscle so that more of the weight lost is fat. Spot reduction of fat from one area is a myth.',
  },
];
---

<CalculatorLayout
  calculator={calc}
  presentation="task-first"
  intro="Estimate your body fat percentage using the U.S. Navy circumference method — just a tape measure and your height. Metric or imperial."
  faqs={faqs}
  reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-07-17' }}
>
  <BodyFatCalculator />

  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use this calculator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Select your <strong>sex</strong> and choose <strong>Metric</strong> or <strong>Imperial</strong> units.</li>
      <li>Enter your <strong>height</strong>, <strong>neck</strong> and <strong>waist</strong> measurements — and, for women, your <strong>hip</strong> measurement.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Calculate</strong> to see your estimated body fat percentage and its category.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What body fat percentage tells you</h2>
    <p>
      Body fat percentage is the share of your total weight that is fat rather than muscle, bone
      and water. Two people at the same weight — or the same BMI — can have very different
      compositions, and it is the fat share that tracks most closely with health. Because it
      separates fat from lean mass, it is a more informative figure than weight alone.
    </p>

    <h2>Where to measure</h2>
    <p>The Navy method needs only a tape measure. Keep it snug but not compressing the skin, breathe normally, and measure at these points:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Neck:</strong> just below the larynx, with the tape sloping slightly downward to the front.</li>
      <li><strong>Waist:</strong> at the navel for men; at the narrowest point for women.</li>
      <li><strong>Hips (women):</strong> at the widest part of the buttocks.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Take each measurement twice and average the two for a steadier result.</p>

    <h2>The U.S. Navy formula</h2>
    <p>
      The method estimates body fat from body circumferences and height using a logarithmic
      formula. The calculator works in centimetres (imperial entries are converted first) and
      uses a different equation for each sex:
    </p>
    <p><strong>Men: 495 / (1.0324 − 0.19077 × log₁₀(waist − neck) + 0.15456 × log₁₀(height)) − 450</strong></p>
    <p><strong>Women: 495 / (1.29579 − 0.35004 × log₁₀(waist + hip − neck) + 0.221 × log₁₀(height)) − 450</strong></p>
    <p>
      The men's version keys off the gap between waist and neck; the women's version adds the hip
      measurement, which improves the estimate where fat is more often carried.
    </p>

    <h2>A worked example</h2>
    <p>
      Take a man who is <strong>180 cm</strong> tall with a <strong>38 cm</strong> neck and a
      <strong>90 cm</strong> waist. The waist-minus-neck term is 52 cm, so log₁₀(52) ≈ 1.716 and
      log₁₀(180) ≈ 2.255. The denominator works out to 1.0324 − 0.19077 × 1.716 + 0.15456 × 2.255
      ≈ 1.0536, giving 495 ÷ 1.0536 − 450 ≈ <strong>19.8%</strong> — the "Average" band for men.
    </p>

    <h2>Interpreting your result</h2>
    <p>Typical reference ranges differ by sex:</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Category</th><th>Men</th><th>Women</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Essential fat</td><td>Below 6%</td><td>Below 14%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Athletes</td><td>6–13%</td><td>14–20%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Fitness</td><td>14–17%</td><td>21–24%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Average</td><td>18–24%</td><td>25–31%</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Obese</td><td>25% and above</td><td>32% and above</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
      Essential fat is the minimum the body needs for normal function; the athlete and fitness
      bands reflect regular training; and the ranges rise from there. Women carry more essential
      fat than men, which is why every band sits higher.
    </p>

    <h2>How accurate is it?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>It is an estimate.</strong> The Navy method can be off by several points for an individual versus a clinical measurement such as a DEXA scan.</li>
      <li><strong>Technique matters.</strong> A tape held too tight or too loose, or placed differently each time, shifts the result — consistency is what makes trends meaningful.</li>
      <li><strong>Body shape affects it.</strong> Because it relies on circumferences, unusual proportions can push the estimate high or low.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Track the trend over weeks rather than fixating on a single reading. To compare with a
      height-and-weight screen, see the <a href="/health/bmi-calculator">BMI calculator</a>; to
      plan a calorie deficit or surplus, use the
      <a href="/health/calorie-calculator">calorie calculator</a>. This is educational
      information, not medical advice.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
